/*
  # NOTE (Sprint 3b, Embedding Explorer): connect-src is widened so transformers.js
  # can download on-device embedding model weights (ONNX) straight from the
  # Hugging Face Hub / its LFS CDN. Sprint 4 (security hardening) should review
  # this — either tighten to a narrower/pinned set of hosts once the exact CDN
  # hosts in use are confirmed, or move to self-hosted model weights under
  # /public and drop these origins entirely.
  # NOTE (Embedding Explorer audit fix): script-src carries 'wasm-unsafe-eval'
  # because Chrome (and other CSP-strict browsers) require it for ALL WebAssembly
  # instantiation — onnxruntime-web boots via WASM even when the WEBGPU backend
  # is selected (it's the runtime host, not just the CPU fallback). Without it
  # the embedding model fails to load for every visitor, silently or not.
  # worker-src already allows 'blob:' below for Vite's `?worker` bundles.
  # cdn.jsdelivr.net is ALSO required (both script-src, for the dynamic
  # `import()` of onnxruntime-web's WASM loader `.mjs`, and connect-src, for
  # the `.wasm`/`.mjs` fetches themselves) — onnxruntime-web's default
  # `wasmPaths` point at jsDelivr, not Hugging Face, regardless of which
  # model is selected. `blob:` is required in script-src too: onnxruntime-web
  # fetches that `.mjs` as text, wraps it in a same-origin `Blob`, and
  # `import()`s THAT blob: URL (a workaround for cross-origin module-script
  # restrictions) — without `blob:` in script-src the blob-wrapped import
  # itself gets refused even once the jsDelivr fetch succeeds. All three
  # pieces (wasm-unsafe-eval + jsDelivr host + blob:) were verified
  # necessary and sufficient with a headless-Chrome run of the actual
  # production CSP against /lab/embeddings/.
  #
  # NOTE (security hardening): connect-src includes the Synapse backend origin
  # https://api.vkvstudio.com — this MUST match the PUBLIC_SYNAPSE_API_URL used for
  # the Cloudflare Pages build, or every chat/voice/tts/stream fetch is CSP-blocked and
  # the site silently degrades to the mock assistant. Update BOTH together if the API
  # host changes. object-src 'none' + base-uri 'self' + form-action 'none' block
  # plugin/base-tag/form-hijack vectors (there are no <object>/<form> on the site).
  #
  # NOTE (2026-09-21, script-src WITHOUT 'unsafe-inline'): the old note here said
  # 'unsafe-inline' was required because Astro's island runtime is inline and a
  # static _headers cannot nonce it. Half right. It cannot be nonced, but it CAN
  # be hashed: a build was scanned for every <script> without src (36 pages,
  # 420 script tags, JSON-LD excluded — JSON-LD is data, script-src never applies
  # to it), and the whole site ships exactly FIVE distinct inline scripts, none
  # of them page-varying:
  #   'sha256-Q27LY9eI…'  the Trusted Types default policy, BaseLayout <head>
  #                       (src/lib/trusted-types-policy.ts, 3376 B, 34 pages —
  #                       2026-09-21: the regex tag/handler check was replaced
  #                       by a parse-and-rebuild allowlist sanitizer, which is
  #                       why this grew from 769 B to 2902 B and why the hash
  #                       changed; a second refutation pass the same day took it
  #                       to 3376 B — it now parses in FRAGMENT context via a
  #                       <template> (the sink's own context), rebuilds kept
  #                       elements with createElement so an `is=` value cannot
  #                       ride along, and RESOLVES URLs instead of matching the
  #                       scheme with a regex)
  #   'sha256-SaCkFfPr…'  Astro's <astro-island> runtime (4403 B, 34 pages)
  #   'sha256-BF0290pk…'  Astro's client:idle loader (316 B, 34 pages)
  #   'sha256-Q2BPg90Z…'  Astro's client:visible loader (372 B, 12 pages)
  #   'sha256-eIXWvAmx…'  Astro's client:only loader (130 B, /lab/embeddings/)
  # Every component <script> (HeroCanvas, Header, PageHero, the pages) is hoisted
  # by Astro into a hashed /_astro/*.js module, covered by 'self'. There is no
  # is:inline or define:vars script anywhere in src/, and the built HTML carries
  # no on*= handler attributes and no javascript: hrefs (grep-verified), so
  # nothing else depended on 'unsafe-inline'. The two pages without the policy
  # hash (/index.html redirect shell, /404.html) have no islands and no sinks.
  #
  # The four Astro hashes are the sha256 of Astro's OWN prebuilt runtime strings
  # (node_modules/astro/dist/runtime/{server/astro-island,client/*}.prebuilt.js)
  # and therefore change whenever Astro changes those files — i.e. on an
  # upgrade. tests/unit/csp-headers.test.ts re-derives all five hashes from the
  # installed Astro and from the policy constant on every `vitest run` and fails
  # with the replacement values, so an upgrade cannot silently ship a header
  # that kills hydration on every page. That test is the regeneration procedure.
  #
  # NOT 'strict-dynamic'. Lighthouse suggests it, and it was measured here
  # (wrangler pages dev, 2026-09-21): 'strict-dynamic' makes the browser IGNORE
  # host sources and 'self', and only propagates trust from a hashed script to
  # scripts that script creates itself. Astro's hoisted modules are ordinary
  # parser-inserted <script type="module" src="/_astro/…"> tags with no hash and
  # no integrity attribute, so every one of them is refused and the page ships
  # dead — the same result the 2026-08-09 preview-deploy experiment recorded in
  # astro.config.mjs. Making it work would mean an SRI hash on every emitted
  # script tag (a post-build rewrite Astro does not offer) plus the jsDelivr
  # blob: import chain re-verified. The host allowlist is the honest fit for a
  # static Astro site; the residual "allowlist bypass" Lighthouse warns about
  # (a script injected from an allowed host) is what the Trusted Types policy
  # below closes at the DOM sink.
  # Lighthouse's remaining Medium note ("consider adding 'unsafe-inline' for
  # backward compatibility — ignored by browsers supporting hashes") is declined
  # on purpose: every browser since CSP Level 2 (2015) ignores it once a hash is
  # present, so it would only ever re-enable inline scripts on a browser too old
  # to matter, and tests/unit/csp-headers.test.ts fails if it comes back.
  #
  # style-src KEEPS 'unsafe-inline' and must: Svelte SSR emits style="" attributes
  # (hero parallax, per-frame values), Astro inlines every stylesheet as a <style>
  # block (build.inlineStylesheets), and GIS injects its own. Per the CSP spec a
  # single hash in style-src would switch 'unsafe-inline' off, so no style hashes
  # are listed either. Nothing in the audit set penalises style-src.
  #
  # require-trusted-types-for 'script': every innerHTML / DOMParser / Worker(url)
  # / script.src / eval write must carry a Trusted Type, and a `default` policy
  # supplies them. The policy is the hashed inline script above, registered
  # before any sink runs; it allowlists the closed tag set the site's own
  # sanitizers emit and the script-src hosts, and refuses everything else — see
  # src/lib/trusted-types-policy.ts for the audit of every sink on the site. No
  # `trusted-types <names>` directive on purpose: Svelte 5 registers its own
  # `svelte-trusted-html` policy for component templates and Google Identity
  # Services registers Closure `goog#html` ones; naming the allowed policies
  # would break both.
  # NOTE (Google sign-in): Google Identity Services powers Synapse conversation
  # ownership. It needs https://accounts.google.com in script-src (the gsi/client
  # loader), connect-src (token + JWKS fetches), and frame-src (the sign-in button
  # + One Tap render inside a Google iframe). Avatar images come from
  # https://*.googleusercontent.com → img-src. All are inert until a client id is
  # configured (PUBLIC_GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID). See src/lib/auth.ts.
  # NOTE (2026-08-09): style-src carries https://accounts.google.com because
  # Google Identity Services loads its own stylesheet (accounts.google.com/gsi/style)
  # for the sign-in button. 'unsafe-inline' does NOT cover an EXTERNAL sheet, so
  # without this host the button renders unstyled or fails outright. Google's own
  # setup guide states this directive requirement.
  # NOTE (2026-08-13): media-src carries blob: because that is the ONLY way the
  # assistant's speech can play. /api/tts returns audio as a Blob, synapse-audio.ts
  # turns it into a blob: URL via createObjectURL, and an <audio> element loads
  # that. With a bare `media-src 'self'` the browser refused it —
  # "Loading media from 'blob:…' violates the following Content Security Policy
  # directive: media-src 'self'" — and the failure surfaced only as
  # "NotSupportedError: Failed to load because no supported source was found",
  # which reads like a codec problem and is not one. Text-to-speech was therefore
  # broken on the DEPLOYED site from the day this header was added, while working
  # perfectly in dev, where Cloudflare's _headers are not applied at all. blob: is
  # same-origin by construction, so this grants nothing an attacker can reach.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-Q27LY9eIKgxX9LhG+ONLtn7lmsZ0gwopA0midEGvJxo=' 'sha256-SaCkFfPruIdTXT8/97JArQmGxiJAL2o4bBDvSgJ5y3Q=' 'sha256-BF0290pkb3jxQsE7z00xR8Imp8X34FLC88L0lkMnrGw=' 'sha256-Q2BPg90ZMplYY+FSdApNErhpWafg2hcRRbndmvxuL/Q=' 'sha256-eIXWvAmxkr251LJZkjniEK5LcPF3NkapbJepohwYRIc=' 'wasm-unsafe-eval' blob: https://cdn.jsdelivr.net https://accounts.google.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://accounts.google.com; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://*.googleusercontent.com; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.vkvstudio.com https://accounts.google.com https://huggingface.co https://*.hf.co https://cdn-lfs.huggingface.co https://cdn-lfs-us-1.huggingface.co https://cdn.jsdelivr.net; worker-src 'self' blob:; media-src 'self' blob:; frame-src https://accounts.google.com; frame-ancestors 'none'; require-trusted-types-for 'script'
  # Kept for ancient user agents only. frame-ancestors above is what actually
  # enforces this — per OWASP, X-Frame-Options is obsolete and ignored wherever
  # frame-ancestors is present. 20 bytes, no harm, no illusions.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  # Blocks this origin's assets from being embedded by other sites.
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  # same-origin-allow-popups (not bare same-origin): the Google sign-in popup must
  # keep its `window.opener` link to postMessage the credential back. Bare
  # same-origin severs it and the sign-in callback never fires.
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  # microphone=(self): the Synapse voice assistant calls getUserMedia({audio}) from
  # the top-level document (src/lib/synapse-audio.ts). An EMPTY allowlist microphone=()
  # disables the mic for EVERY context incl. same-origin → voice input breaks. camera/
  # geolocation/payment stay fully disabled (unused).
  # usb/serial/display-capture denied too — the site never uses them, and an
  # explicit denial is what a scanner looks for. Do NOT add interest-cohort or
  # browsing-topics here: those features were removed and now only emit
  # "Unrecognized feature" console warnings.
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(self), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), serial=(), display-capture=()
  # `preload` was claimed here but vkvstudio.com was never submitted to
  # hstspreload.org (verified 2026-08-09: status "unknown"), so the token
  # asserted something untrue. Dropped rather than left as decoration.
  # Submitting is also a one-way door — with includeSubDomains it would pin
  # api.vkvstudio.com to HTTPS-only before that host even exists.
  # UPDATE 2026-08-15: api.vkvstudio.com has been live since 13 August, and
  # includeSubDomains above already pins it for anyone who has visited the apex.
  # What is still missing is on the ZONE, not here: api.* answers plain HTTP with
  # real JSON and sends no HSTS of its own, because the apex's 301 comes from
  # Pages and the tunnelled host never inherits it. Turn on "Always Use HTTPS"
  # and HSTS at the Cloudflare zone; preload submission can follow once that is
  # true for every host under the domain.
  # UPDATE 2026-09-21: `preload` is back, and this time it is a request, not a
  # claim. hstspreload.org requires the token in the header BEFORE it accepts a
  # submission (max-age ≥ 1 year and includeSubDomains, both already here), so
  # the header has to carry it first. Submitting at https://hstspreload.org is
  # the OWNER'S separate action and has not been done by this change. Once the
  # domain is in the Chromium list it is effectively a one-way door: every host
  # under vkvstudio.com — api.vkvstudio.com (Cloudflare Tunnel) included — must
  # answer HTTPS forever, because browsers will refuse plain http:// to any of
  # them before a single request is sent, and removal takes months to propagate.
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload

# Content-hashed build output. Pages' default for these is
# `public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate` — four hours, then a revalidation
# round-trip for a file whose name changes whenever its bytes do. The hash IS
# the cache key, so a year and `immutable` cost nothing and remove the
# conditional request entirely. Lighthouse flags the default as an inefficient
# cache policy, and on a site that treats 100 as the baseline that is a free
# point.
#
# This block sits AFTER /* on purpose: Cloudflare Pages does not pick the most
# specific rule, it applies every matching block in order, so a later block is
# what wins. Deliberately scoped to /_astro/* — the videos, icons and fonts in
# public/ keep their plain names across rebuilds and must NOT be immutable.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The self-hosted fonts carry stable, non-hashed names (inter-latin.woff2, …),
# so they cannot be `immutable` the way /_astro can — a rebuild reuses the name.
# But Pages' 4-hour must-revalidate default costs a returning visitor a
# conditional request on every preloaded font, and a font that changes gets a
# new @font-face `src` in the hashed CSS anyway, so 30 days is safe and removes
# the revalidation. Same reasoning for the videos and the PWA icons below them:
# plain names, rarely changed, and a change ships new markup that points at it.
/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000
# Thirty days at the edge and in browsers, which means a video re-encoded under
# the SAME name is invisible for up to a month: on 2026-09-21 the 6.6 MB
# hero-scroll replaced a 25.7 MB one and Cloudflare kept serving the old bytes
# (cf-cache-status HIT, age 2.4 days) until the file shipped as
# hero-scroll.v2.mp4. Every re-encode ships under a NEW filename; the reference
# lives in HeroCanvas.astro (hero) and SynapseBrain.svelte (orb).
/*.mp4
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000

# Static machine-readable guides and discovery manifests.
/llms.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/agent-skills/*
  Content-Type: text/markdown; profile="urn:air:agent-skills"; charset=utf-8

/.well-known/*.json
